```java
BufferedReader br = null;
try {
    br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"));
    return br.readLine();
} finally {
    if (br != null) {
        br.close();   // can ITSELF throw, hiding the real exception
    }
}
// Easy to forget the null check or the close entirely -> resource leak.
```

```java
try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    return br.readLine();
}
// close() is called automatically on the way out, even if readLine() throws.
// If close() also fails, it's attached as a suppressed exception.
```
*Less code, no null check, and the original exception is never hidden by a failing `close()`.*

```java
public class DbConnection implements AutoCloseable {
    DbConnection() { System.out.println("opened"); }
    void query(String sql) { System.out.println("run: " + sql); }

    @Override
    public void close() {            // narrowed: declares no checked exception
        System.out.println("closed"); // release the underlying resource here
    }
}

// Usage -- close() runs automatically, even if query() throws:
try (DbConnection db = new DbConnection()) {
    db.query("SELECT 1");
}
// prints: opened, run: SELECT 1, closed
```

